\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods (naive plug-in, MLA, DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023}) across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations; full results in Table~\ref{tab:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
Under linear OLS, for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate, FWL implies that any linear transformation of $\mathbf{D}^*$ preserves the column space relative to $T$, leaving the treatment coefficient unchanged: naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction (Appendix~\ref{app:fwl_proof}). This result is specific to linear models; under logistic regression the invariance breaks, but empirical breakdown is negligible ($< 0.05\%$ for covariate topologies across $N$ up to 50{,}000; Appendix~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}). All structural patterns---sign-flip rates, topology rankings, and FWL invariance for covariate topologies---persist under logistic DGP ($|\Delta\,\text{SF}_\tau| < 0.02$; Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp}).

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean RMSE reduction (47.0\%) but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%). MC-SIMEX provides gold-free correction (best for IV at 59.4\%) but harms front-door ($-$62.4\%). For protective topologies, correction re-exposes the original bias pathway. No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent.

\paragraph{Case studies and sensitivity.}
ClinicalBERT validation (Appendix~\ref{app:case_study}) confirms FWL invariance in clinical NLP. PPI++ requires ${\geq}20\%$ gold labels for IV but harms M-bias at low gold ratios ($-125\%$ RMSE at 5\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:ppi_gold_ratio}). An ASV case study on political ad tone ($K{=}3$, exposure; Appendix~\ref{sec:asv_case_study}) shows $3{\times}$ higher error tolerance when the annotated variable is a confounder rather than the exposure.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Setup and Notation}
\label{sec:notation}

Let $A$ denote a true $K$-class categorical variable (e.g., sentiment with $K{=}3$ levels) and $A^*$ its observed, potentially misclassified version produced by an LLM annotator. The misclassification process is characterized by a $K{\times}K$ confusion matrix $C$ with entries $C_{jk} = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k)$, where each column sums to one. Following \citet{hernan_cole2009}, we represent this measurement process using \emph{starred-node} DAGs: the true variable $A$ is latent, and the observed $A^*$ is connected to $A$ via a dashed measurement edge.

\begin{definition}[Non-differential misclassification]
\label{def:nondiff}
Misclassification of $A$ is non-differential with respect to outcome $Y$ and covariates $\mathbf{Z}$ if $P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k, Y, \mathbf{Z}) = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k) = C_{jk}$ for all $j,k$.
\end{definition}

This holds in standard annotation pipelines where the LLM receives only source text and classifies without observing the outcome or covariates---the typical setup in computational social science~\cite{ziems2024can}. Violations arise when pretraining data encodes $A$--$Y$ associations, outcome-conditional prompting is used, or retrieval pipelines surface $Y$-correlated documents. Our stress test (Appendix~\ref{sec:nondiff_stress}) shows uniform violations below $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ cause $<$3~pp degradation; outcome-conditional violations can exceed uniform predictions while label-specific violations are milder. When non-differential misclassification is violated for $K{\geq}3$ exposure, even $\varepsilon{=}0.01$ structured departure produces sign reversal in 14 of 16 empirical configurations (Appendix~\ref{sec:realistic_epsilon}).

We study seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy})~\cite{pearl2009} in which $A$ occupies structurally distinct causal roles: confounder, exposure, mediator, collider, M-bias variable, front-door mediator, and instrument. Throughout, we derive probability limits under \emph{linear} structural equation models~\cite{carroll2006}, the standard framework for exact characterization of topology-dependent bias; robustness under logistic regression is verified in Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp} and~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}.

\subsection{Multi-Class Bias Expressions}
\label{sec:bias_expressions}

For each topology, we derive the $\text{plim}$ (probability limit) of the naive estimator that substitutes $A^*$ for $A$. Let $\tau$ denote the target causal parameter---its precise definition is topology-specific (e.g., the coefficient on $X$ for confounding, the category-specific $\beta_j$ for exposure, or the direct effect $\tau_{\text{direct}}$ for mediation)---and $\hat{\tau}(A^*)$ the estimator using misclassified annotations.

\paragraph{Confounding ($A$ = confounder).}
When $A$ confounds the $X{\to}Y$ relationship, the OLS estimator controlling for $A^*$ has probability limit
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:confounding}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(A^*) = \bigl(\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}Z]\bigr)^{-1}\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}X]\,\boldsymbol{\beta},
\end{equation}
where $Z = [X, \text{dummies}(A^*)]$, $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ contains the true coefficients, and the target parameter $\tau$ is the coefficient on $X$. Misclassification produces residual confounding that monotonically approaches the omitted variable bias, but the sign of $\hat{\tau}$ is preserved: $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$ structurally.\footnote{This result is consistent with \citet{brenner1993}, who showed that the adjusted estimate with misclassified polytomous confounders can be biased toward or away from the null. Both findings hold simultaneously: misclassification degrades confounding control, producing residual bias whose magnitude can exceed the correctly adjusted estimate (amplification), but the treatment effect sign is never reversed. The distinction is between \emph{sign reversal} ($\text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)$, which we show never occurs) and \emph{amplification} ($|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$, which occurs universally in this topology).}

\paragraph{Exposure ($A$ = treatment variable).}
When $A$ is the exposure of interest, the biased coefficient for category $j$ becomes a weighted combination of true coefficients:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exposure}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\beta}_j(A^*) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_{jk}\,\beta_k,
\end{equation}
where $w_{jk}$ depends on the confusion matrix $C$ and the population marginal $\pi_k = P(A{=}k)$. Unlike the binary case, where non-differential misclassification guarantees attenuation toward the null~\cite{brenner1993}, the $K{\geq}3$ case permits $w_{jk}$ to assign negative weight to categories with opposite-sign coefficients, producing \emph{sign reversal}. A near-permutation confusion matrix effectively swaps category labels, making sign reversal the dominant outcome (83.4\% under uniform Dirichlet prior). This failure mode is structurally impossible at $K{=}2$ because a $2{\times}2$ stochastic matrix with positive diagonal cannot reverse the sign of a single coefficient.

\paragraph{Numerical Verification 2} (Primary coefficient preservation).
Across 23 empirical LLM configurations, the primary coefficient (largest $|\beta_k|$) never reverses sign, while 61\% reverse a secondary coefficient. This reflects magnitude dominance: under 100{,}000 Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ matrices with diagonal ${\geq}\,0.7$, primary sign-flip rate is 0\%; without a diagonal constraint, both coefficients flip ${\sim}50$\% (detailed breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:exposure}).

\paragraph{Mediation ($A$ = mediator).}
When $A$ mediates the $X{\to}Y$ relationship, the direct effect estimator controlling for $A^*$ has probability limit
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mediation}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}_{\text{direct}}(A^*) = \tau_{\text{direct}} + \bigl(1 - \lambda(C)\bigr)\,\tau_{\text{indirect}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(C) \in [0,1]$ under proportional effects (Appendix~\ref{app:proof}, Proposition~\ref{prop:lambda_bound}), with $\lambda(I) = 1$ under perfect classification. Misclassification ($\lambda < 1$) causes the residual indirect effect to be absorbed into the direct path estimate, producing upward bias in $|\hat{\tau}_{\text{direct}}|$ but preserving the sign ($\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$).

\paragraph{Collider, M-bias, and front-door.}
Three topologies exhibit \emph{protective} or \emph{immune} behavior. \textbf{Collider:} conditioning on $A^*$ instead of $A$ attenuates the spurious path, so annotation error reduces collider bias ($\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$). \textbf{M-bias:} in the M-shaped DAG~\cite{greenland2003}, misclassifying $A$ weakens the M-bias path; this topology is immune ($\text{ASV} = \infty$). \textbf{Front-door:} the front-door estimator~\cite{bellemare_bloem2024} is attenuated in both $X \to A$ and $A \to Y$ stages, producing moderate bias with $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$.

\paragraph{Instrumental variable ($A$ = instrument).}
The Wald estimator~\cite{angrist_imbens1995} $\hat{\tau}_{\text{IV}} = \hat{\text{cov}}(A^*, Y) / \hat{\text{cov}}(A^*, X)$ has $C$ in the denominator. Misclassification can drive first-stage coefficients toward zero or reverse their sign, causing the ratio estimator to diverge or flip. Under random Dirichlet matrices, 52.6\% produce sign reversal, making IV the most fragile topology (detailed analysis including the Dirichlet subspace geometry in Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}).

\paragraph{Structural sign-flip guarantee.}
\begin{proposition}[Sign preservation---analytic results]
\label{prop:signflip}
Under non-differential misclassification with $C_{kk} > 0$ for all $k$:
\emph{(a)}~Confounding preserves sign when $\operatorname{sign}(\tau) = \operatorname{sign}(\tau {+} B_{\mathrm{OV}})$, where $B_{\mathrm{OV}} {=} \operatorname{Cov}(g(A),f(A))/\operatorname{Var}(T)$ is the omitted-variable bias (Cauchy--Schwarz; Appendix~\ref{app:confounding}).
\emph{(b)}~Collider preserves sign when $|\tau| > \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(g)\operatorname{Var}(h)}/\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(T{\mid}A)]$ (Berkson attenuation; Appendix~\ref{app:collider}).
\emph{(c)}~Exposure can produce sign reversal for $K {\geq} 3$, even with $C_{kk} {>} 0.5$ (counterexample in Appendix~\ref{app:exposure}).
\end{proposition}

Our theoretical results establish a three-tier hierarchy: (i)~analytically proved (Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip}(a,b), Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}), (ii)~numerically verified with zero violations across 20{,}000+ random configurations (Observation~\ref{prop:monotone_general}, Numerical Verifications~1--2), and (iii)~conjectured with $\varepsilon$-approximate bounds (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}).

\paragraph{Numerical Verification 1} (Mediation, M-bias, front-door).
Sign preservation holds under topology-specific conditions (mediation: $\operatorname{sign}(\tau_{\mathrm{dir}}) {=} \operatorname{sign}(\tau_{\mathrm{total}})$; front-door: $\operatorname{sign}(\lambda\alpha_U) {=} \operatorname{sign}(\lambda\alpha_U {+} \operatorname{Cov}(T,f(A)))$; M-bias: attenuating for all $C$). Verified across 100{,}000 random matrices with zero violations (Appendix~\ref{app:conditions}); analytic proofs for general $K$ remain open. Table~\ref{tab:sign_conditions} (Appendix) summarizes all topology-specific sign preservation sufficient conditions. Since these conditions involve true DGP parameters, the ASV framework (§\ref{sec:asv}) provides a complementary assessment not requiring their knowledge.

\subsection{Annotation Sensitivity Values}
\label{sec:asv}

How much annotation error can a study tolerate before conclusions change? We adapt the sensitivity analysis paradigm---E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017}, partial-$R^2$ bounds~\cite{cinelli2020making}---to $K{\times}K$ annotation error, defining Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs). Our approach: (1)~prove $\varepsilon$-approximate monotonicity along a 1D path $C(\delta)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}), reducing the $K^2$-dimensional problem to a scalar threshold; (2)~define three ASV diagnostics addressing topology-dependent failure modes; (3)~combine ASVs with FWL invariance to route practitioners to topology-appropriate corrections.

\paragraph{Parameterization.}
We parameterize the confusion matrix as $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, where $C_0$ is a reference confusion matrix and $\delta \in [0,1]$ controls severity ($\delta{=}0$: perfect; $\delta{=}1$: matches $C_0$). Non-negativity requires $\delta \leq \delta_{\max} = \min_j 1/(1 - (C_0)_{jj}) \geq 1$; we recommend bootstrapping $C_0$ and reporting ASV confidence intervals (Appendix~\ref{sec:bootstrap_asv}).
\paragraph{Three ASV types.}
We define three complementary measures: (1)~\textbf{Magnitude ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{mag}} = \min\{\delta : |\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(C(\delta)) - \tau| > \epsilon\}$---analogous to E-values; (2)~\textbf{Significance ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{sig}} = \min\{\delta : |\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(C(\delta))| < z_{\alpha/2} \cdot SE(N)\}$---relevant for attenuating topologies; (3)~\textbf{Sign-flip ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{sf}} = \min\{\delta : \text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)\}$---exposure and IV only. The significance ASV depends on $N$ through $SE(N)$, exhibiting a sharp phase transition at a critical $N^*$ below which all rejections are blocked.

\begin{theorem}[Envelope monotonicity---$K{=}2$]
\label{thm:monotone}
For $K{=}2$ confounding and mediation topologies with proportional effects ($f = \lambda g$ for some scalar $\lambda$) under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$ with $(C_0)_{jj} \geq \tfrac{1}{2}$, $|B(C(\delta))|$ is monotonically non-decreasing in $\delta \in [0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

The proof (Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof},~\ref{par:med_monotone}) exploits perspective function convexity; $(C_0)_{jj}{\geq}\tfrac{1}{2}$ excludes degenerate matrices. Proportional effects ($f = \lambda g$) holds when a single latent factor drives both paths. Under non-proportional effects, $\lambda(C)$ exceeds $[0,1]$ in up to 23\% of random configurations, but sign preservation remains ${\geq}97$\% even then. Validation with 23 real LLM confusion matrices confirms $\lambda(C) \in [0,1]$ for ${\geq}99$\% of configurations under proportional DGPs with 100\% sign preservation (Table~\ref{tab:proportional_empirical}, Appendix~\ref{sec:proportional_effects},~\ref{sec:proportional_real_matrices}).

\begin{observation}[Envelope monotonicity---$K{\geq}3$]
\label{prop:monotone_general}
For $K{\geq}3$ under the same conditions, numerical verification across 20{,}000 random $(C_0,p,g)$ triples per $K$ shows worst-case deviations $< 0.005$ from strict monotonicity (Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof}).
\end{observation}

\begin{conjecture}[$\varepsilon$-approximate envelope monotonicity]
\label{conj:monotone}
For non-IV topologies under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, $|B(C(\delta))|$ is monotonic up to $\varepsilon = 0.06$ deviations across all tested configurations (collider/M-bias non-increasing, others non-decreasing). Verified across 30{,}000 random matrices per topology, $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, $\tau \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\}$; per-topology bounds in Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify}.
\end{conjecture}

Unlike E-values, which quantify unmeasured confounding strength via a scalar sensitivity parameter with trivially monotonic bias~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017}, ASVs parametrize $K{\times}K$ annotation error along a topology-specific path where monotonicity requires proof (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}) or numerical verification (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). The sign-flip ASV has no E-value counterpart, and the vulnerability ranking (IV most fragile, M-bias immune) is a function of DAG structure absent from confounding sensitivity analyses~\cite{rosenbaum2002}.

For $K{=}2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone} guarantees exact monotonicity, making ASV thresholds well-defined. For $K{\geq}3$, monotonicity rests on numerical evidence (Observation~\ref{prop:monotone_general}, Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). The proof obstacle is structural: the $K{=}2$ argument exploits perspective function convexity on scalar $(N_j, q_j)$ pairs; at $K{\geq}3$, bias paths traverse a $(K^2{-}K)$-dimensional manifold where off-diagonal perturbations interact through ratio structures (mediation) and denominator crossings (IV), precluding element-wise decomposition.

Under $\varepsilon$-approximate monotonicity, $\delta^*$ precision depends on the local slope $\eta = |dB/d\delta|$: confounding, mediation, and front-door yield $\delta^*$ precision within 0.12; exposure and IV require bootstrap CIs (Appendix~\ref{sec:bootstrap_asv}). Rankings are stable across DGP parameterizations (Appendix~\ref{sec:asv_stability}). Given $C_0$, if sign-flip ASV $< 1$, the topology--coefficient pair is flagged as sign-reversal-prone; if magnitude ASV $< \delta_{\text{observed}}$, bias exceeds tolerance (Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

Binarization of $K{=}3$ annotations introduces additional topology-dependent bias (Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}).

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The transition at $K{\geq}3$ is not gradual but structural. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with positive diagonal to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension~\cite{brenner1993}. This guarantee \emph{vanishes} at $K{\geq}3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even under high accuracy~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. Under a uniform Dirichlet prior, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ exposure confusion matrices produce sign reversal of at least one secondary coefficient; under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 2.1\% still do. The primary treatment coefficient, however, is never reversed in any tested configuration---an empirical regularity we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

Standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%). $F_1$ shows no significant rank correlation with causal bias across 56 HumAID scenarios (Spearman $\rho = -0.10$, $p = 0.45$), confirming that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001} noted the possibility of polytomous sign reversal; \citet{brenner1993} identified amplification for polytomous confounders; \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology. We present the first unified analysis of $K$-class annotation error propagation across seven canonical DAG topologies, building on prior single-topology analyses~\cite{brenner1993, teblunthuis2024}. Our analytical predictions match Monte Carlo estimates within 1.6\% MAPE across 161 scenarios.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification in linear structural equation models, prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door, and show that exposure and IV can produce sign reversal. Binarization amplifies the gap: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies.\footnote{Computed as the ratio of maximum to minimum binarization-induced bias across seven topologies under $K{=}3$ Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ confusion matrices; see Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}.}

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values.} As a corollary of the bias taxonomy, we define three complementary ASV diagnostics---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip---revealing a topology-dependent vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile; 52.6\% sign reversal under random Dirichlet prior, though zero reversals occur under empirical LLM matrices) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods and prove a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result: matrix-based corrections achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate---a negative result overlooked in the annotation correction literature. PPI++ achieves 47.0\% RMSE reduction but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
The foundations are established in \citet{carroll2006}, \citet{gustafson2004}, \citet{buonaccorsi2010}, and \citet{bound2001measurement}, with starred-node DAGs formalized by \citet{hernan_cole2009} and DAG-based correction theory developed by \citet{kuroki_pearl2014}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases toward the null~\cite{brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}, with \citet{neuhaus1999} quantifying the attenuation and efficiency loss in binary regression under known misclassification rates; but this fails for polytomous exposures~\cite{dosemeci1990, jurek2005, veirod_laake2001}. \citet{edwards_lessler_naimi2021} showed dependent misclassification can bias in either direction, and \citet{shu_yi2019a, shu_yi2019b} develop weighted estimators that jointly handle mismeasured covariates and misclassified outcomes under IPW---a concern that also drives mediation analyses with mismeasured mediators~\cite{valeri_vanderweele2013}. All analyze a single DAG topology; ours is the first to characterize how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation.}
\citet{grimmer2022} and \citet{feder_etal2022} provide foundations for text-as-data and causal inference in NLP. \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to classifier error; \citet{teblunthuis2024} gave the most complete binary treatment ($K{=}2$, four DAGs); \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only confounding. On annotation quality, \citet{gilardi2023chatgpt} and \citet{ziems2024can} document the rapid adoption of LLM annotators, \citet{baumann2025} found 31\% incorrect conclusions, \citet{pangakis_wolken_fasching2023} demonstrated that automated annotations require task-specific validation, \citet{cheng_mayya_sedoc2024} propose structured approaches to reduce LLM measurement error, \citet{variance_aware_llm2026} develop variance-aware annotation protocols, \citet{hidden_measurement_error2026} characterizes hidden measurement error in LLM pipelines, and \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models. \citet{audinet2025} benchmark debiasing; \citet{keith_jensen_oconnor2020} survey the broader landscape. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose quality without connecting it to causal validity.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and correction methods.}
Our ASV framework draws on the sensitivity analysis without assumptions of \citet{ding_vanderweele2016}, E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017, mathur2018}, the robustness value of \citet{cinelli2020making}, and the mediation sensitivity analysis of \citet{imai_keele_yamamoto2010}, but parametrizes annotation error severity rather than omitted variable strength or sequential ignorability violations. The quantitative bias analysis framework of \citet{lash2009} provides a complementary epidemiologic perspective, and \citet{mahajan2006} establishes identification of regression models with misclassified regressors via instrumental variables. The unified measurement-error and missing-data framework of \citet{blackwell_honaker_king2017} similarly motivates treating annotation noise as a first-class object of inference. For correction, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} are topology-agnostic; \citet{zrnic_candes2024} extend PPI to gold-free settings, \citet{demirel2024prediction} generalize prediction-powered inference to causal parameters across populations, and \citet{cadei2025} adapt it to treatment-effect targets via a deconfounded risk objective, and \citet{gligoric2025} leverage LLM confidence scores. Our contribution reveals that this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective for four topologies. Bayesian measurement error models~\cite{richardson1993bayesian, gustafson2004} and EM imputation offer alternatives that bypass FWL invariance.

